# Prefer match/case

Use structural pattern matching when branching on a single discriminant — enums, literals, or typed model variants — instead of long `if`/`elif`/`isinstance` chains.

```python
# ❌ BAD — isinstance chain
if isinstance(spec, OnceTrigger):
    ...
if isinstance(spec, CronTriggerSpec):
    ...
if isinstance(spec, IntervalTriggerSpec):
    ...

# ❌ BAD — literal elif chain
if output.type == "cron":
    ...
elif output.type == "interval":
    ...
elif output.type == "once":
    ...

# ✅ GOOD — match on type or enum
match spec:
    case OnceTrigger(run_at=run_at):
        ...
    case CronTriggerSpec(expression=expression, timezone=tz):
        ...
    case IntervalTriggerSpec(seconds=seconds):
        ...
    case _:
        raise ValueError(f"Unsupported trigger type: {spec}")

# ✅ GOOD — match on string literal / enum
match output.type:
    case "cron":
        ...
    case "interval":
        ...
    case "once":
        ...
```

Use `case` guards for extra conditions on a branch:

```python
match meridiem:
    case "pm" if hour != 12:
        hour += 12
    case "am" if hour == 12:
        hour = 0
```

Keep plain `if` for one-off boolean checks, early returns, and conditions that are not dispatch on a shared value.
